From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aditya kale <252700881+kaleaditya28897-linux@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:41:08 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] malloc: tst-memalign-3 without malloc built-ins (clang folds
 its comparisons)

tst-memalign-3 frees ten chunks and checks that the next memalign
returns one of them, comparing the freed pointers with the new one.
Clang treats malloc, memalign and free as built-ins and folds an
equality between two allocations' results to false, so the count is 0
and the test fails though the allocator returned a freed chunk. Built
without those built-ins, as tst-tcfree3 already is.  Joist, Phase 5
step 7: glibc's tests on Joist, the one failure left unexplained.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 malloc/Makefile | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/malloc/Makefile b/malloc/Makefile
index bd7383d..15e705c 100644
--- a/malloc/Makefile
+++ b/malloc/Makefile
@@ -452,6 +452,11 @@ CPPFLAGS-malloc.c += -DUSE_TCACHE=1
 #CPPFLAGS-malloc.o += -DMALLOC_DEBUG=1
 
 CFLAGS-tst-tcfree3.c += -fno-builtin-malloc -fno-builtin-free
+# tst-memalign-3 compares pointers it has freed with what memalign returns
+# next; clang folds those comparisons to false (a fresh allocation never
+# equals another), so the test fails where the allocator is right.
+CFLAGS-tst-memalign-3.c += -fno-builtin-malloc -fno-builtin-memalign \
+			   -fno-builtin-free
 
 sLIBdir := $(shell echo $(slibdir) | sed 's,lib\(\|64\)$$,\\\\$$LIB,')
 
